WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.810 --> 00:00:06.329
140 chickens get processed every minute in
most poultry plants in the US.

00:00:06.329 --> 00:00:09.567
That’s the maximum speed the US government
allows most poultry plants

00:00:09.567 --> 00:00:11.752
to run their assembly lines.

00:00:11.752 --> 00:00:15.494
It’s a speed that helps chicken companies
stay profitable.

00:00:15.494 --> 00:00:20.142
But if you ask poultry workers, they’ll
say it’s a speed that causes them pain.

00:00:26.632 --> 00:00:30.191
Meat processing is a dangerous job in America.

00:00:30.191 --> 00:00:33.051
Workers are 3 times more likely 
to get seriously injured

00:00:33.051 --> 00:00:35.099
than the average American worker. 

00:00:35.099 --> 00:00:39.297
More likely to get seriously injured than
people who work in oil and gas drilling

00:00:39.297 --> 00:00:43.516
industrial building construction, 
or people who operate sawmills.

00:00:43.516 --> 00:00:48.847
And that was before the COVID pandemic 
tore through meat plants.

00:00:48.847 --> 00:00:52.614
Meat processing has a long history 
of safety issues.

00:00:52.614 --> 00:00:56.414
Using a sharp tool quickly and
 close to other people is dangerous.

00:00:56.414 --> 00:01:01.360
But some of the harm is avoidable, because
today, workers are without some basic protections

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.568
necessary to keep them safe.

00:01:08.020 --> 00:01:12.718
So why has the US failed to protect the people
who process our meat?

00:01:15.962 --> 00:01:22.010
Meat processing refers to the stages of turning
a live animal into a food product in a factory.

00:01:22.010 --> 00:01:25.251
For poultry, a factory layout 
might look something like this.

00:01:25.251 --> 00:01:28.551
Workers hang live chickens by their feet on
a conveyor belt

00:01:28.551 --> 00:01:32.102
where machines stun them, 
cut their throats, defeather them

00:01:32.102 --> 00:01:35.028
remove their heads and feet, 
and take out their guts.

00:01:35.028 --> 00:01:40.171
Then their carcasses are sanitized with an
acid solution before they get stored in a cooler.

00:01:40.171 --> 00:01:43.624
This slaughtering section is 
almost entirely automated

00:01:43.624 --> 00:01:46.089
and it's the fastest part of the line.

00:01:46.089 --> 00:01:49.504
But what happens next still requires
human precision.

00:01:49.504 --> 00:01:51.526
Unless they are sold as whole chickens

00:01:51.526 --> 00:01:55.122
workers cut up the carcasses into parts, by hand.

00:01:55.122 --> 00:01:57.592
This process is called deboning.

00:01:57.592 --> 00:02:00.000
That’s the job this worker has. 

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:03.067
She works at a poultry plant in 
northwest Arkansas.

00:02:03.067 --> 00:02:07.600
And requested to remain anonymous for fear 
of retaliation from her company.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:10.717
She's worked in poultry processing
for over 20 years.

00:02:10.717 --> 00:02:14.119
And it’s how she met her husband, 
who died last year from COVID

00:02:14.119 --> 00:02:17.391
after the virus spread through 
poultry plants in the area.

00:02:50.442 --> 00:02:53.287
Deboning work is extremely repetitive.

00:02:53.287 --> 00:02:56.560
She's making those motions 
thousands of times a day.

00:02:56.560 --> 00:03:02.540
A 2014 CDC report found that, 34% of workers
suffer from carpal tunnel syndrome

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:07.663
and 76% of workers had nerve disorders 
when working at standard speeds.

00:03:07.663 --> 00:03:12.800
These conditions make the finger joints enlarged
and stiff, and give workers wrist pain

00:03:12.800 --> 00:03:15.381
like these photos of poultry workers’ hands show.

00:03:32.479 --> 00:03:38.549
Pace, repetition, and musculoskeletal disorders
also increase the odds of more severe injuries

00:03:38.549 --> 00:03:42.313
which is why the poultry industry has more
reports of finger amputations

00:03:42.313 --> 00:03:44.189
than any other industry.

00:03:44.189 --> 00:03:49.763
And why the meat processing industry as a
whole averages 2 amputations a week.

00:03:49.763 --> 00:03:54.452
Americans eat a lot more meat per capita today
than we did in the 60s.

00:03:54.452 --> 00:03:58.097
But we actually eat less beef 
and just a little more pork.

00:03:58.097 --> 00:04:00.503
It’s because we eat a lot more chicken.

00:04:00.503 --> 00:04:03.419
One reason is because chicken is comparatively cheap.

00:04:03.419 --> 00:04:06.499
It’s less than half the cost of pork or beef.

00:04:06.499 --> 00:04:10.969
If a meat processing company wants to keep
prices low and its profits high

00:04:10.969 --> 00:04:13.017
they can adjust their output.

00:04:13.017 --> 00:04:17.556
In other words, they can try to produce more
chicken with the same cost of labor.

00:04:17.556 --> 00:04:21.269
That can be achieved through faster 
slaughter line speeds.

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:25.371
The US Department of Agriculture regulates
meat processing line speeds.

00:04:25.371 --> 00:04:28.394
But it’s primarily concerned with food safety.

00:04:28.394 --> 00:04:33.809
Another agency, the Occupational Safety and
Health Administration, regulates worker safety

00:04:33.809 --> 00:04:37.242
but doesn’t have the power to set 
line speed limits.

00:04:37.242 --> 00:04:40.571
Line speeds affect both food and worker safety

00:04:40.571 --> 00:04:45.486
but regulating them through the USDA means
worker safety risks may be overlooked.

00:04:45.486 --> 00:04:47.580
KELLOWAY: It's based on food safety standards.

00:04:47.580 --> 00:04:52.866
It does not consider the effect 
on workers at all.

00:04:52.866 --> 00:04:57.840
In 2001, an OSHA “ergonomics rule went into
effect” “aimed at reducing injuries…

00:04:57.840 --> 00:05:00.776
caused by a worker performing a repetitive
task,”

00:05:00.776 --> 00:05:04.139
which could have empowered the agency 
to regulate line speeds.

00:05:04.139 --> 00:05:06.733
But within a year, Congress 
“axed” the rule after

00:05:06.733 --> 00:05:09.449
“business groups argued the standards 
were too costly.”

00:05:11.905 --> 00:05:16.025
Today, the National Chicken Council, the lobby
that represents the poultry industry

00:05:16.025 --> 00:05:20.522
routinely asks the US government 
to increase the maximum line speed.

00:05:20.522 --> 00:05:26.337
Starting in 2018, the USDA granted waivers
to at least 54 plants to increase their line speeds

00:05:26.337 --> 00:05:30.469
from 140 birds per minute to 175.

00:05:30.469 --> 00:05:34.826
These 15 plants received those line speed
increases in 2020

00:05:34.826 --> 00:05:38.844
even though they had been previously cited 
for worker safety violations

00:05:38.844 --> 00:05:40.693
and for severe injuries

00:05:40.693 --> 00:05:44.250
according to a report by the 
National Employment Law Project. 

00:05:44.250 --> 00:05:46.933
Evidence that worker safety 
may not have been a consideration

00:05:46.933 --> 00:05:49.099
when allowing faster speeds.

00:05:49.099 --> 00:05:53.714
KELLOWAY: Humans are not machines 
and there's just a limit to

00:05:53.714 --> 00:05:56.653
human capacity for some of these 
repetitive motions.

00:05:56.653 --> 00:06:01.037
It’s not just line speeds that fall into
this gap in regulatory oversight.

00:06:01.037 --> 00:06:04.505
It’s also why the amount of chemicals used
to prevent food-borne illness

00:06:04.505 --> 00:06:07.377
like peracetic acid, ammonia, and chlorine

00:06:07.377 --> 00:06:09.840
aren’t regulated for worker safety.

00:06:09.840 --> 00:06:16.527
Chemical accidents like ammonia and chlorine
gas leaks injure hundreds of workers a year. 

00:06:16.527 --> 00:06:18.884
And they frequently make workers sick.

00:06:36.391 --> 00:06:39.515
Absent stricter government regulation 
to protect them

00:06:39.515 --> 00:06:44.773
workers may feel they only have 
one other option: unionization.

00:06:44.773 --> 00:06:48.180
But that’s become out of reach 
for most workers.

00:06:53.312 --> 00:06:56.944
LICOLLI: I believe it's a mess.

00:06:56.944 --> 00:07:02.163
Magaly Licolli organizes non-unionized 
poultry workers in northwest Arkansas

00:07:02.163 --> 00:07:04.639
through an organization called Venceremos.

00:07:05.590 --> 00:07:09.080
She’s digging through her storage container
to find protest signs.

00:07:09.080 --> 00:07:12.894
LICOLLI: This was when we were telling them

00:07:12.894 --> 00:07:17.220
to shut down the factories because of the outbreaks.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:20.391
This part of the country is crucial 
for poultry production.

00:07:20.391 --> 00:07:25.533
These three counties in Arkansas process more
poultry than almost anywhere in the US.

00:07:25.533 --> 00:07:28.409
And multiple poultry companies 
have plants here.

00:07:28.409 --> 00:07:32.562
Including Tyson, one of the largest poultry
producing companies in the world

00:07:32.562 --> 00:07:36.343
which has its corporate headquarters here
in Springdale, Arkansas.

00:07:37.205 --> 00:07:41.470
That’s where Magaly is headed, to deliver
a petition in protest of working conditions

00:07:41.470 --> 00:07:44.650
at a poultry plant also located in Springdale.

00:07:45.070 --> 00:07:47.462
LICOLLI: Workers have been complaining a lot.

00:07:47.462 --> 00:07:52.385
The working conditions have gotten worse,
even worse than the pandemic.

00:07:52.385 --> 00:07:56.811
LICOLLI: There is less workers and workers
have to work faster.

00:07:56.811 --> 00:08:00.230
LICOLLI: And the salary was the same.

00:08:00.230 --> 00:08:04.750
The petition asks for a $4/dollar an hour
wage increase, no restrictions on bathroom

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:09.837
and water breaks, more frequent short breaks,
and paid sick leave.

00:08:09.837 --> 00:08:13.620
When we reached out to Tyson for this story,
they wrote that they have “no reason to

00:08:13.620 --> 00:08:15.470
deny breaks” to workers

00:08:15.470 --> 00:08:19.789
and claimed the petition was “misrepresented” 
to the workers who signed it.

00:08:19.789 --> 00:08:23.156
Venceremos organizes poultry workers from
other companies in the area

00:08:23.156 --> 00:08:25.804
who have had similar complaints

00:08:25.804 --> 00:08:30.229
like this strike at a George’s poultry plant 
in Springdale, Arkansas. 

00:08:30.229 --> 00:08:34.133
The work that Magaly’s organization does
is important in a state like Arkansas

00:08:34.133 --> 00:08:38.719
where the vast majority of poultry workers 
don’t have access to a union.

00:08:40.445 --> 00:08:45.895
Unions have historically made dangerous jobs,
like slaughterhouses, a little safer.

00:08:45.895 --> 00:08:52.743
But in the 1980s, union power declined, 
in part due to a rise in anti-union politics.

00:08:52.743 --> 00:08:55.330
Those trends hit the meat industry, too.

00:08:55.330 --> 00:09:02.950
KELLOWAY: Unions once played a really important
watchdog role in both maintaining a wage floor

00:09:02.950 --> 00:09:07.811
across the industry and maintaining 
safety standards.

00:09:07.811 --> 00:09:12.692
Pork and beef processing companies shuttered
unionized plants located in urban areas

00:09:12.692 --> 00:09:16.692
and reopened non-union plants 
in rural areas.

00:09:16.692 --> 00:09:20.262
To meet this skyrocketing demand for chicken
starting in the 80’s

00:09:20.262 --> 00:09:24.385
the poultry industry set up large plants 
in rural areas, too.

00:09:24.385 --> 00:09:29.064
Arkansas and other top poultry-producing states
have anti-union laws

00:09:29.064 --> 00:09:32.851
and have some of the lowest 
union membership in the country.

00:09:32.851 --> 00:09:36.684
Meat processing workers are about twice as
likely to be a person of color 

00:09:36.684 --> 00:09:39.114
to not be a citizen, and to be foreign-born,

00:09:39.114 --> 00:09:41.459
when compared to the total workforce.

00:09:41.459 --> 00:09:46.107
LICOLLI: The majority of the workers working
in the northwest Arkansas are from

00:09:46.107 --> 00:09:49.838
Central America, a lot of people 
from Mexico

00:09:49.838 --> 00:09:53.000
but also one of the biggest populations 
is also people

00:09:53.000 --> 00:09:55.263
from the Marshall Islands.

00:09:55.263 --> 00:09:59.291
When unions began to decline 
between 1980 and ‘90s

00:09:59.291 --> 00:10:03.040
injuries in the meatpacking sector rose by 40%.

00:10:03.040 --> 00:10:06.130
Since then, government data 
shows that injuries have declined

00:10:06.130 --> 00:10:09.862
a statistic the National Chicken Council 
shows on their site

00:10:09.862 --> 00:10:13.786
and roughly the same one they gave us 
when we reached out to them for comment.

00:10:13.786 --> 00:10:17.603
But there’s a debate about how reliable
that data is.

00:10:17.603 --> 00:10:21.450
KELLOWAY: There's a lot of evidence that shows
reporting is down considerably.

00:10:21.450 --> 00:10:25.917
There are a lot of worker-led and advocate-led surveys

00:10:25.917 --> 00:10:30.507
that show persistent high levels of injury.

00:10:30.507 --> 00:10:34.187
One of these surveys was given to Alabama
poultry workers.

00:10:34.187 --> 00:10:36.123
72% of them said they suffered from

00:10:36.123 --> 00:10:39.596
“significant work-related 
injury or illness.”

00:10:39.596 --> 00:10:44.898
That’s compared to 4.5% in the government’s
data on the whole country from that same year.

00:10:51.931 --> 00:10:56.921
Because I've seen workers suffering, 
you know, all these years.

00:10:56.921 --> 00:11:02.345
They’ve had accidents, injuries that left
them disabled for life.

00:11:02.345 --> 00:11:09.250
If they have babies, sometimes it's hard for
them to hold them because of the issues that

00:11:09.250 --> 00:11:11.550
they have in their hands.

00:11:11.550 --> 00:11:15.850
The poultry industry could make these workers’
lives a little easier—and, importantly,

00:11:15.850 --> 00:11:19.097
safer by making modifications to this line.

00:11:19.097 --> 00:11:23.531
Like reducing the speed, giving workers more
breaks, or minimizing repetition

00:11:23.531 --> 00:11:25.705
by switching up job roles.

00:11:25.705 --> 00:11:29.970
Paid sick leave is another improvement and
Tyson recently announced they would start

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:33.222
offering this benefit to workers in 2022 

00:11:33.222 --> 00:11:35.470
but only the vaccinated ones.

00:11:37.580 --> 00:11:40.872
For now, Magaly continues her fight 
on behalf of workers.

00:11:58.707 --> 00:12:02.069
We went outside the Tyson headquarters.

00:12:02.069 --> 00:12:08.467
The director of public relations was there
to receive the petitions.

00:12:08.467 --> 00:12:12.968
I think that people really need to take into
consideration that

00:12:12.968 --> 00:12:16.025
without these workers we don't have 
food on our table.

00:12:16.025 --> 00:12:18.416
And that's just facts.

00:12:18.416 --> 00:12:24.640
It's just not only about the humanity but the
moral duties that we have to these workers.

00:12:30.856 --> 00:12:36.522
Hi, thanks for watching this second episode
of our Future Perfect series on the meat industry.

00:12:36.522 --> 00:12:40.769
Something that didn’t make it into this
story is the impact that this book had.

00:12:40.769 --> 00:12:42.893
This is The Jungly by Upton Sinclair.

00:12:42.893 --> 00:12:47.500
It's a novel that was published in 1906 about
the misfortunes of a Lithuanian family who

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:50.790
immigrates to the US to work in Chicago’s
meatpacking industry.

00:12:50.790 --> 00:12:54.171
It was a best-seller of its day 
and it was hugely influential.

00:12:54.171 --> 00:12:58.321
Theodore Roosevelt who was president at the
time even credited the book for

00:12:58.321 --> 00:13:02.299
inspiring him to pass the first 
food safety regulation in the US.

00:13:02.299 --> 00:13:06.120
But Sinclair apparently had hoped that the
focus would have been more on the workers

00:13:06.120 --> 00:13:08.684
and the animals suffering in this system.

00:13:08.684 --> 00:13:10.918
I talked to Claire Kelloway about this.

00:13:10.918 --> 00:13:13.129
KELLOWAY: Sinclair, the author of The Jungle, 
will often say

00:13:13.129 --> 00:13:16.529
“I was aiming for readers’ hearts and 
hit them in the stomach."

